[FINAL-TEXT] Direct evidence confirmed on router6. Three FORWARD DROP rules on eth2 (`-i eth2 -p tcp`, `-i eth2 -p udp`, `-i eth2`). eth2 = r6_r3 link to router3 (backbone). This blocks all inter-site traffic.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** iptables FORWARD chain on router6, specifically three DROP rules gating its eth2 interface (the r6_r3 backbone link toward router3 and the wider network).

**Mechanism:** The three rules — `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`, and `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP` — drop every packet that arrives at router6 on eth2, regardless of protocol. This creates a bidirectional blackout for the 172.18.51.x site: inbound traffic from other sites (pc11–14 on 192.168.200.x, pc4/pc5 on 172.16.10.x) travels router3 → router6 eth2 and is immediately discarded. Outbound packets from pc15–18 leave via eth0/eth1 toward mswitch2/switch10 fine, but response traffic returns via eth2 and is dropped — so even self-initiated sessions appear completely broken.

**Localization:** router6, eth2 (collision domain r6_r3, the direct link to router3). mswitch2, switch10, and the 172.18.51.x hosts themselves are all healthy — intra-subnet reachability among pc15–18 is confirmed at 0% loss.

**Root cause:** Three iptables FORWARD DROP rules on router6 block all forwarded traffic received on eth2, isolating the 172.18.51.x site from every other subnet in the topology.

**Fix:** On router6, remove the three rules:
```
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP
```
=== END ===